\section{Methods}
This section describes the methodological framework used to analyze, implement,
and experimentally evaluate the Suprakernel Sovereign Architecture. The methods
combine manifold-based mathematical modeling, autonomous kernel mutation,
telemetry-driven stability enforcement, and distributed GPU execution across a
64-node HGX H100 cluster.

\subsection{System Modeling and State Representation}
The system state is represented as a vector $p(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ evolving on
the Riemannian stress manifold $\mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}} = (\mathcal{U}, g)$.
The $\Omega$-mapping operator projects raw state vectors into the manifold:
\begin{equation}
\Omega : \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}},
\end{equation}
with Jacobian determinant $J(p)$ used to monitor geometric stability.

The governing dynamics are:
\begin{equation}
\dot{p}(t) = \mathcal{F}(p) - \nabla V(p) - \mathbf{D}(p),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{F}(p)$ is the forcing term, $V(p)$ is the potential function,
and $\mathbf{D}(p)$ is an adaptive damping operator satisfying:
\begin{equation}
\langle \mathbf{D}(p), p \rangle \ge c_d \|p\|^2.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Lyapunov and Barrier Function Construction}
To ensure stability, we define the Lyapunov candidate:
\begin{equation}
L(p) = \frac{1}{2}\|p - p^*\|^2 + U(p) +
\frac{1}{2}\gamma(t)\max(0,-J(p))^2,
\end{equation}
where $U(p)$ is the stress potential and $p^*$ is the equilibrium state.

The safe region is defined by the barrier function:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C} = \{ p \mid h(p) \ge 0 \},
\end{equation}
with forward invariance enforced by:
\begin{equation}
\dot{h}(p) + \gamma_{\text{CBF}} h(p) \ge 0.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Autonomous Kernel Mutation Procedure}
Kernel evolution is governed by the four-stage mutation loop:
\[
\text{Observe} \rightarrow \text{Orient} \rightarrow \text{Mutate} \rightarrow \text{Verify}.
\]

\paragraph{Observe.}
The system ingests state vectors and telemetry:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}(t) = \{ J(p), \alpha(t), \beta(t), U(p),
B_{\text{mem}}, U_{\text{tensor}} \}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Orient.}
The state is projected onto $\mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}}$, and the system
computes:
\begin{equation}
J(p), \qquad \nabla U(p), \qquad (\alpha - \beta),
\end{equation}
to assess geometric and dynamical stability.

\paragraph{Mutate.}
The JIT compiler proposes kernel updates $\Delta K$ subject to:
\begin{equation}
\|\Delta K\| \le \kappa_{\max}(t),
\end{equation}
where $\kappa_{\max}(t)$ is derived from real-time telemetry.

\paragraph{Verify.}
A mutation is accepted only if:
\begin{equation}
\dot{L}(p) < 0, \qquad
J(p) > 0, \qquad
\dot{h}(p) + \gamma_{\text{CBF}} h(p) \ge 0.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Cluster Execution Environment}
All experiments were conducted on a 64-node NVIDIA HGX H100 cluster with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item synchronized CUDA streams,
    \item NVLink 4.0 interconnect,
    \item RDMA-enabled MPI channels,
    \item sovereign telemetry daemon.
\end{itemize}

Each node executes CUDA/PTX kernels under manifold-aware scheduling and
stability constraints. Distributed synchronization ensures that Jacobian values,
Lyapunov margins, and mutation decisions remain globally consistent.

\subsection{Stress Testing Protocol}
Stress tests were performed by varying:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ingestion bandwidth (1.5--60 GB/s),
    \item tensor core utilization (50--99.2\%),
    \item Jacobian floor values (1.0--0.005),
    \item rendering latency (8.5--10.9 ms).
\end{itemize}

Telemetry-driven stability enforcement was monitored continuously to verify:
\begin{equation}
\dot{L}(p) < 0, \qquad J(p) > 0,
\end{equation}
under all stress conditions.

\subsection{Data Collection and Analysis}
All telemetry logs, mutation histories, Jacobian trajectories, and Lyapunov
derivatives were recorded at 1 kHz sampling frequency. Data were analyzed using
cluster-wide aggregation and manifold-aware smoothing filters.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
All experiments were repeated across multiple cluster configurations to ensure
reproducibility. Mutation acceptance criteria, stability thresholds, and
telemetry limits were held constant across trials.